  Abstract                 = {Some free software and open source projects have been extremely successful in the past. The success of a project is often related to the number of developers it can attract: a larger community of developers (the â€˜bazaarâ€™) identifies and corrects more software defects and adds more features via a peer-review process. In this paper two free software projects (Wine and Arla) are empirically explored in order to characterize their software lifecycle, development processes and communities. Both the projects show a phase where the number of active developers and the actual work performed on the system is constant, or does not grow: we argued that this phase corresponds to the one termed â€˜cathedralâ€™ in the literature. One of the two projects (Wine) shows also a second phase: a sudden growing amount of developers corresponds to a similar growing output produced: we termed this as the â€˜bazaarâ€™ phase, and we also argued that this phase was not achieved for the other system. A further analysis revealed that the transition between â€˜cathedralâ€™ and â€˜bazaarâ€™ was a phase by itself in Wine, achieved by creating a growing amount of new modules, which attracted new developers.},
  Affiliation              = {University of Lincoln UK},
  File                     = {:Capiluppi_2007.pdf:PDF},
  ISBN                     = {978-0-387-72485-0},
  Keyword                  = {Computer Science},
